 take our data and  our API is and make them as secure as  possible so absolutely nobody can get  access to them but the problem is if  that's our approach to API security and  all of a sudden we lock things down so  much that we can't actually  actually use them we're gonna miss out  on all the value of api's and so we're  trying to actually build out API  security locking things down isn't the  hard part it would be easy enough for us  just to not build an API and that's the  most secure API of all but trying to  foster innovation allowing the people  who can build really interesting things  with our API s and not putting in place  really heavyweight bottlenecks and  unnecessary delays while keeping our API  secure is a much much harder problem for  us to solve and that's what we're going  to talk about here today in this session  so the way that we the way that we put  this talk together is I went and I spent  a lot of time so in my role I spent a  lot of time with customers and I get a  chance to talk with organisations from  you know all around the country all  around the world and I like to sort of  ask them what sort of things they're  concerned about and so what we did is we  we started off by looking at specific  customer examples and how those  customers are addressing their security  needs so we'll start off with a pretty  basic one so we have a lot of retail  customers that we work with at Apogee  and a lot of times they'll have api's  like this these are things like like  store locations and hours so they're not  we're not looking at sensitive  information I mean we want people to  know when our stores are open we want  people to be able to find our store  location so they can come to the stores  when they're open to do purchasing with  us but at the same time it's important  information so we probably have these  type of api's attached to our mobile app  we do want to make sure that people you  know that this API does remain secured  so we don't so that people can actually  use it so what type of things do we have  to be concerned about with an API that's  this like this no sensitive information  but yet relatively important well I know  that in a security talk I've learned  I've done a number of security talks  over the years and one of the things  that I've noticed is that if I don't  mention TLS like it's the very first  thing and yeah I've got some security  people in the audience you get very  service that I didn't mention TLS though  so I'm gonna go ahead and get that out  of the way at the beginning that that  there are some basic things that we want  to do at this point TLS is just sort of  implied that we're always going to put  TLS on top of our our api's so now that  that's out of the way the other things  we have to think about one of the one  customer that I talked with he put it  best he said every time I I on board a  new application I just on boarded a new  potential source of DDoS attack and that  doesn't necessarily mean that people are  you know intentionally ddossing but  somebody could do something incorrectly  they could build an app that all of a  sudden just has more traffic than they  were anticipating and all of a sudden  before you know you have floods of  traffic that are coming into this API  and they end up inadvertently taking it  down and again with something like like  a store locator that means that if  somebody is on your mobile app and  they're trying to get access to your  store or find the closest store suddenly  that customer can can no longer actually  come into the store so we have to think  about things like even with this API  that we want people to have access to we  want them to be able to use it how can  we allow them to use it in a way that  doesn't introduce unnecessary risk  so traditionally our approach to this  type of problem was to enforce some type  of heavyweight governance so before I  joined Apogee before we required by  Google I spent a lot of time working for  a large insurance company in our  approach whenever we wanted to sort of  share data was very much a matter of  picking up the phone or sending an email  saying hey I know you have a service I'd  like to use this in my application and  usually the response was something like  great let's sit down when can we get  together and you can fill out this  hundred question questionnaire about  everything you're going to do with my  data and then we'll understand your  business justification and then we'll  decide if we're going to give you access  or not and over the course of weeks of  getting together and sort of reviewing  and security reviews ultimately you  would get access to the API and for no  very good reason we ended up delaying  our ability to actually get something  out the door so a much better approach  of this is just to enforce traffic  management  there's nothing sensitive about this  information it's just store locations  and hours so  what if we could give access to anyone  who wanted it either within our  organization or outside depending on the  API you're working with and say you can  have access to it automatically will  give you access to this API however  we're gonna limit that access so if you  wanted to make more than say 100 calls  an hour or a thousand calls an hour or  whatever is appropriate we're just going  to start throttling that traffic and  we're not going to give you access so we  don't do worry about the floods of  traffic coming in and inadvertently  doing a denial of service and we can  also open up this API so more people can  use it the other traditional approach  that we would see is something like a  service registry and in this sense I'm  not talking about sort of the service  registries than to come along recently  in a micro services based architecture  I'm talking about sort of the the  old-school service web service  repositories and registries and those  type of things where we would try to  keep track of ok who has access to this  API and we'll sort of note down you know  who might be the potential source of  floods of traffic that are coming in how  much better approaches to automate that  so with with platforms that the API  management platforms usually we can do  things like issue API keys and so if we  wanted to be able to look and see  exactly who's making which calls and how  much Tropics coming in API keys provide  us a great way of doing that now we have  an identifier we can use to track our  traffic that's coming through and to  enforce those quotas so we know that as  an API call comes in it has a key this  is the app this is what the apps  entitled to and we'll start throttling  those things right away the other thing  we have to be aware of our content-based  attacks so even though this particular  API doesn't intentionally expose  sensitive data as somebody may try to  get around that and they may try to send  and request that the API is not  expecting maybe they're doing things  like sequel injection attacks or  cross-site scripting and so we want to  be able to protect against those those  miscreants that are trying to send a  negative you know attacks into our API  so this is usually where we start to  think about the capabilities of an API  gateway and how we can use an API  gateway to intercept those calls and to  actually enforce rules that will protect  against content-based attacks and even  though I don't want to make this a  commercial about Appaji just so happens  that Google has a platform called Appaji  that solves all these security problems  and again  if you want to talk about aperture you  have more questions about your API s and  API security happy to dive into those  details and again these are all customer  examples that we've that we've talked to  and worked with as we put this talk  together so the next the next API that  we talked with so this is actually a an  insurance company and one of the one of  the challenges with with insurance is  t