we  help so the traditional approach was we  just wouldn't we would just work with  the companies who could afford to spend  the time with us to put together you  know a tiger team that's going to go  through and six months later of meetings  and compliance and auditors will feel  confident that they can sell our our  products and others can so what they  found was they can actually use they can  productize api is in such a way that  they can offer them in a self-service  way to these smaller companies so where  as a large company in order to do things  like actually sell up sell an insurance  product you didn't have the same  regulations just to get a quote on an  insurance product so they said what if  what if we could take our api or our  partner api and we could productize that  in such a way that we offer in a  self-service automated fashion anybody  can come and offer a quote and we'll do  a revenue share model with them so that  we can comply with regulations we'll be  the ones that are actually selling the  product not the partner but we can offer  them a way to still comply with  regulations and in doing so what they  what they ended up doing was a it would  enforce fine grained access control so  depending on who you are and who we were  that what's coming into their API  program they could say we're only going  to let you see and get access to these  very specific pieces of our API so we  can still foster the innovation that  we're after but we're only going to give  the full ability to actually sell an  insurance product to our big partners  who are willing to go through this  process with us and when it came to  actually enforcing this at the runtime  perspective what they do is they say  well great when we when somebody signs  up we're going to give you credentials  and you'll use those credentials to  obtain an OAuth token so when an OAuth  token comes in we're going to look and  we're going to say which products do you  have access to do you have access to the  ability to sell the insurance product or  just to get a quote on the insurance  product and so in this way they were  able to take an existing API that they  had built out for their partners and  they were able to repurpose it in such a  that they can now support a tremendous  more a tremendously larger number of  partners now one of the last ones that  we'll talk about here so I was working  with with a large Hospital chain and so  they probably have one of the more  interesting interesting api's that I've  come across so what they've done is they  have wired up their their ICU so you  know they have very very sick patients  that are coming in they've got a lot of  sensors and sort of information that's  constantly flowing back from from the  ICU they've got all the patient's  medical data and they're taking that in  they're building out sort of a risk  assessment so every day if you're a  physician at this hospital and you have  a patient in the ICU it's gonna tell you  here are the patients that are sort of  in the most critical state and here are  the things that we've seen that have  sort of it all led to you know either  either death or negative outcomes that  we should be aware of and here's sort of  a treatment plan and so you can imagine  in this particular case you're dealing  with HIPAA compliant data you're dealing  with you know very sensitive PII data of  course that's coming through and as a  result they had some really major  security concerns so what can what what  should this particular organization be  concerned about well this was by far in  a way their their biggest concern when  you're dealing with HIPAA there are real  legal criminal consequences that you can  run into if all of a sudden you take  take a patient's information in fact one  of the one of the hospitals that that  they run they ended up having some  celebrities coming in and all of a  sudden you had sort of people that  wanted to you know take a sneak peek at  their at their medical records and ended  up finding some people actually in here  so we wanted to make sure nobody ended  up here for no good reason and so one of  the things I want to make sure to  mention during this talk is that  sometimes this approach is absolutely  okay I mean there are going to be times  when we do have incredibly sensitive  information that we're working with and  it's going to be information that  absolutely does have to be locked down  it's not something we can take lightly  that we can allow you know just anybody  to get access to and so I don't want you  walking away from this talk thinking you  know this this guy doesn't understand  our security we're in the financial  industry and we've got you know very we  got people's bank accounts so we need to  do this absolutely it's really more a  matter of one of the key takeaways here  is that it's really a matter of looking  at the api's understanding of the  threats understanding how we can get the  API out as much as possible and  recognizing that sometimes we don't want  it out at all so in this case what they  had to figure out who actually  authorized to access patient data so in  this case it was very clear that they  had depending on on the patient's you  were assigned as a physician they wanted  to make sure you would only see the  information for you know for your  patients and so as a result we had to  have a way for these these doctors and  nurses and other other caregivers to  authenticate and so you know  traditionally one of the problems we  faced and especially in sort of the the  web space is direct user authentication  and we've seen sort of over the years  we've done single sign-on type solutions  and things like that you know home hold  products are sort of sprung up to solve  the SSO problem and so in an API for  from an API perspective we have a  similar construct with a delegated  identity and user authorization so a lot  of times this will be things like open  ID connect where