ct this data and run it  through our analysis engine which  detects anomalies and abuse and then our  sense console offers insights about what  type of traffic is observing and allows  you to review that traffic and take  action on it including blocking it or  flogging it we'll talk about that in a  minute so I'm gonna walk you through  what a common attack may look like for  many customers there's an expectation of  where they operate their business where  traffic should be coming from so maybe  you have some your selling products  online maybe you're a domestic airline  but you typically know where traffic  should be coming from or definitely  where it should not be coming from and  at a glance here you can see that with  the world map is covered in higher than  normal traffic from all over the world  so many countries there may be you not  be allowed to conduct business but  you're seeing a significant amount of  traffic and in the top corner you can  see that the bot traffic has increased  by nearly 30 percent in the in the near  term so clearly there's an attack going  on in this case so as we dive deeper to  investigate what's what's going on we  can start by reviewing the API abuse in  the detection report tool so this this  is where sense analyzes and unless you  act on behaviors that we can see that  that exceed up thresholds that are said  in the product so for example there's a  Brut guesser  rule which in this case was violated  more than thirty five hundred times  thirty five hundred different bots  resulting in millions of malicious API  calls there's a full list of the rules  here that you can see of and the number  of bots that are exceeding those those  thresholds so this is this is gives you  an overview of the different types of  protection that are offered through the  sentence product so in the previous  slide I showed an example of the brute  guess or policy being violated and you  see higher than normal traffic that  results in errors over a period of time  so that may be someone trying to attempt  to login or someone in another examples  the content scraper rule where a very  common attack used to scrape products  and pricing data so if you have mobile  apps for shopping  those api's maybe or web web apps for  the same use case those may be scraped  for pricing product information or again  like log and api's and these types of  things so we have these various  thresholds and sets of rules that offer  protection for those types of attacks so  if we kind of go back to the attack we  can see that all of the API calls we're  going after the login URI endpoint here  so you can see that the highest value  attacks such as purchasing payments  loyalty programs gift cards and login  are the most commonly abused and require  the the highest level of protection  using tools such as cents if we drill  down on the specific IP address to  investigate further we can also see that  most of the calls in this case all of  them were resulting in four ones and 403  s meaning unauthorized access so this is  what triggered the rule in this  particular case so after review I think  we have enough information to make a  decision to block this traffic so Apogee  sense offers you the ability to block  flag allow block or flag the API traffic  so flagging would allow you to add  custom metadata to the headers which may  enable you to configure a honeypot or  other system to deal with the traffic  you're also allowed to block it at this  point to prevent that that IP from being  able to continue making API calls or  maybe you're running a flash sale or  some other  area where you'd explicitly want to  allow this type of traffic but that's  that's in your control so as we can see  here once we took action the malicious  traffic stopped and we're constantly  working on adding new methods of  detection across our API products Google  is making a very large investment in  security as you may have seen from other  sessions at next and